 { Initially there was going to be a full chapter in this course by the heading of 'Common Pawn Types'. The idea had been to cover the different types of pawns and the many strategies that have been developed around them. From isolated pawns (including the IQP) to doubled pawns, from backward pawns to hanging pawns to pawn wedges to connected passed pawns, and some others. Unfortunately, it slowly dawned on me that there was no way to cover the material comprehensively in a single chapter. It is a world in itself, and something that would require a full course dedicated to it. My suggestion to any students who wish to delve deeper into this world would would be to get their hands on a book that centres around this. One name that comes to mind is Andrew Soltis' 'Pawn Structure Chess', while for structures more generally I highly recommend 'Chess Structures: A Grandmaster Guide' by GM Mauricio Rios Flores, available on Chessable. As for this course, we'll dedicate this section to studying a small number of typical strategies based around some of the most common pawn types. Let's begin with a technique involving fixing backward pawns. } 1... { -KEY- } Nd7 $1 { Black knows that White is not in great shape to stop the plan of turning White's b-pawn into a backward pawn. } ( 1... a4 { Black is not yet ideally placed to lock in the backward pawn, since here after } 2. Qd1 $1 { Black would be forced to defend the pawn by playing } Ra8 { While Black maintains an advantage, it's not ideal to have to return with the rook to the a-file. } ) 
2. Rb1 ( 2. b3 $1 { The attempt to block Black's plan was perhaps best, but after } Qd6 $1 3. Ra2 Nc5 4. Rb1 { White has been forced to go passive with both rooks, just to avoid a backward pawn. This is too great of an investment of resources, and after a quiet move like } b6 { Black stands a lot better, with enduring pressure against the pawns. It's worth nothing that the move } 5. b4 { is not possible due to the outpost on c3, when after } Na4 $19 { Black will land a knight on c3 next, winning the exchange. } ) 
a4 $1 { Fixing the backward pawn on b2. } 3. Rdd1 ( 3. b4 { The problem with 'removing' the backward pawn is that after } axb3 $1 4. Rxb3 Nc5 $17 { Black has found an ideal weak square for their knight on c5, while the a3 pawn is left permanently weak. } ) 
Nc5 $1 { The knight is ideally placed on c5, where it can never be bothered since the move b4 will always be met by en passant. } 4. Nd2 Na5 $17 { Black has a vice-like grip on the b3 square, and stand much better with more space in both the centre and queenside. With the support of their powerful knights Black can keep steadily improving their position, perhaps even expanding on the kingside with ...f5 at the right moment. } 
