 { Sometimes it's also important to 'lock in' the backward pawn before trying to capture it. In this case, Black's pawn on d6 is struggling to advance. Can you spot the move that Magnus Carlsen played against GM Zhigalko from here? } 1. { -KEY- } e4 $1 { Correct! Now the d6 pawn is stuck, while White intends to follow up with 2.Bh3 to target the rook on d7. } ( 1. Bh3 $6 { straight away would have lost a chunk of the advantage. Here Black could have continued with } e4 $1 { when after } 2. Rd4 f5 $13 { Suddenly, the game hangs in the balance since Black will be able to play ...d5 next. } ) 
f5 { This may have been Black's best bet. } ( { In the game, Black chose } 1... Qa7 $2 { but after } 2. Bh3 Rb7 3. Rxd6 $18 { White had picked up the pawn, and was already completely winning. } ) 
2. exf5 $1 Nxf5 3. Bh3 $1 ( { The pin against the knight is very problematic. } 3. Bh3 g6 { is not possible since after } 4. Bxf5 gxf5 5. Qxh6 { White wins a pawn on the kingside. } ) 
Rf7 4. Bxf5 Rxf5 5. Rxd6 $18 { In the end the pawn falls anyway. White is winning since not only are they a pawn to the good, but they also have a better structure and control of the open file. This brings us to the end of our first technique on fixing backward pawns. Next up, we'll take a look at a technique around doubled pawns. } 
