 { Our next technique is about finding good opportunities to get rid of doubled pawns. Can you find the best move for Black in this position? } 1... { -KEY- } c4 $1 { Correct! Black takes the opportunity to advance the pawn, knowing that sooner or later White will have to play either b3 or d3, forcing undoubling of the c-pawns. } ( 1... Qc7 $2 { A quiet move would miss the opportunity to undouble the pawns. Here White could continue with either 2.d3 or 2.c4. } 2. d3 $14 { White has a slightly better position due to their better structure. } ) 
2. d3 { White tries to finish their development, but now Black gets an ideal change to the structure. } ( 2. b3 { Challenging the pawn with a flank pawn may have been best, but here White's exposed king could prove problematic: } Nf6 $1 3. e5 Qb6 $1 4. Kh1 Ng4 $15 { The position remains very messy, but Black seems to have the upper hand with a lot of pressure against White's structure in both the centre and queenside. } ) 
cxd3 $1 3. Qxd3 Qb6+ 4. Kh1 d5 $1 { Not the only way to play, but I like this energetic approach. Note that the exchange of the c- and d-pawns have helped Black to increase their control over the light squares, so breaking with ...d5 makes sense to increase this lead. } 5. exd5 Ba6 $1 6. c4 cxd5 $1 7. Qxd5 Rd8 8. Qe4 Nf6 $17 { For the cost of just one pawn, Black has built up a nice initiative and a significant lead in development. White will have to play very precisely to avoid their position degrading rapidly. Scouting for opportunities to undouble your pawns is a good practice. Pouncing on those moments of carelessness from the opponent can allow you to remove your only positional problem (in this case doubled pawns) and then push for an advantage by using whatever positional assets you had obtained in exchange for the worse structure (in this case the bishop pair). Let's take a look at another technique around doubled pawns next. } 
