 { The technique seen in this position, in the online game Carlsen,M - Theodorou,N (2022) is also commonly encountered and very powerful. } 1. { -KEY- } e5 $1 { White spots that Black has doubled pawns on the c-file, and so targets the d6 pawn. I refer to this as 'splitting the chain' and it's a very powerful technique as it can turn reasonably good doubled pawns into the worst kind: doubled and isolated! } dxe5 2. fxe5 Bg7 { Unable to capture on e5, Black retreats with the bishop. } ( 2... Bxe5 $2 { allows for a thematic exchange sacrifice with } 3. Rxe5 $1 Nxe5 4. Bc3 f4 5. Re1 Rf5 6. Rxe5 $1 { A second exchange sacrifice! } Rxe5 7. Qxf4 $18 { White is completely winning, since they will be left with two minor pieces against the Black rook. } ) 
 ( 2... Nxe5 $2 { runs into big problems after } 3. Qg3 $1 g5 4. Bc3 $1 f4 { Black rushes the pawns forward in order to break the pin, but here White has a beautiful tactical idea. } 5. Qxg5 $3 Bxg5 6. Bxe5 Qxe5 7. Rxe5 $18 { White has recovered his sacrificed queen, while Black's structure is left completely ruined. } ) 
3. Qf2 $1 { Immediately pressuring the now very weak isolated pawn on c5. } Rb8 4. Nxc5 Nxc5 5. Qxc5 $18 ( { White is a full pawn to the good with a better structure, leaving them with a winning position. In fact, from here the game lasted only one more move. Black played } 5. Qxc5 Rfd8 $2 { and resigned before White played his move, likely spotting that } 6. Ba5 $1 { would win an exchange. Splitting the pawn chain is a very powerful technique that can allow you to turn the opponent's relatively healthy structure into a game-losing positional problem. Next up, let's introduce one more technique based around doubled pawns. } ) 

