 { One of the techniques that arise when there are doubled pawns (or the potential for doubled pawns) on the board is the pawn sacrifice. In this position, we can see that White's d4 pawn is under fire. } 1. { -KEY- } O-O $1 { White ignores the central pawn. } ( 1. Be3 $6 { A move such as this one would seem to make the most sense, but after } cxd4 $1 2. Bxd4 Nc6 $13 { Black has benefited from White's reactiveness to the pressure against d4. Now White has to spend more time moving their bishop back. The position still remains better for them but it is less convincing than the game continuation. } ) 
cxd4 2. Nb5 $1 { This is the point! White understands that the doubled pawns on the d-file are static and it is nothing like sacrificing a healthy central pawn. By playing in this way they have been able to build up their initiative very quickly, paving the way for a quickfire kingside attack. } Nc6 3. f5 $1 { Offering up a second pawn on e5. Perhaps surprisingly, the attack is so strong that White is already winning! } Nxe5 { Black grabs the second pawn, not so much out of greed but out of desire to strip White of the dangerous advanced pawns. } ( { The computer suggests the move } 3... Kd7 { as offering the best chance, but abandoning the kingside will not work against best play. White can win with a move like } 4. Qh5 { when after } g6 5. fxe6 fxe6 6. Bxg6 $18 { White uses tactics to recover their missing pawn, with Black's king remaining in permanent trouble. } ) 
4. Qh5 $1 Nxd3 5. fxe6 g6 6. exf7+ Kd7 { The king begins its attempt to flee. } 7. Qg4+ Kc6 8. cxd3 $18 { In the end the dust has settled and White has recovered both of their sacrificed central pawns. The Black king will not survive for long on c6. This pattern can be seen in all stages of the game. Let's check out one more example, this time played in an endgame position. } 
