 { When facing an isolated queen's pawn, or IQP, it's important to recognise that the extra central control that the IQP offers does allow for dynamic possibilities like a kingside attack. For this reason, the most powerful method for defusing such pawns is to force the exchange of pieces. By reducing the material, you take away attacking prospects while making it easier to exploit the structural weakness of the IQP. } 1... { -KEY- } Nxe4 $1 { The first set of minor pieces is exchanged. } 2. Bxe7 { White has to agree to a second piece exchange, since the bishop was hanging. } Nxe7 $1 { Recapturing with the knight forces further piece exchanges. } ( 2... Nxf2 $6 { is tempting, but gets too messy after } 3. Bxh7 $1 ) 
3. Bxe4 Bxe4 { Black has achieved their goal of reducing the material and they enjoy a pleasant long-term edge due to White's isolated queen's pawn on d4. Let's see how play might continue. } 4. Rxe4 Rc8 5. h3 Qd5 6. Re1 Rfd8 $17 { White is permanently on the back foot, having to babysit their weak IQP and with no attacking prospects. Chess players describe such positions as a 'two-result game', a way of commenting that the Black side runs little practical risk of losing. } 
