 { The first technique that we'll take a look at here is based around isolated pawns. When your opponent has such a pawn, it pays to take note of the square in front of that pawn. This square will by definition always be at least a weak square, making it ideal to occupy with one of your pieces. } 1... { -KEY- } Nd7 $1 { Black steps the knight to d7 to increase their control over the e5 square. } 2. Rae1 Qe5 $1 { The queen takes up residence on e5. } ( 2... Ne5 { would be another equally strong approach. } ) 
3. Qh4 { White understandably wants to keep the queens on the board, since an exchange would leave them with the unenviable task of having to defend a worse position due to their isolated pawn. } Nc5 { The problem for White is that keeping the queens on the board is not very helpful when the opponent's queen is far stronger than your own! } 4. Kg1 Kg8 { Black patiently improves every piece, including the king. } 5. Re3 Rad8 { Given the opportunity, Black will double up rooks on the d-file when the bishop on d3 will be in trouble. } 6. Qf4 { A testament to the strength of Black's queen! White has nothing better than to finally agree to an exchange. } Qxf4 7. Rxf4 Rd7 $17 { Black stands clearly better, with superior pieces and a more compact structure. } 
