 { The square in front of an isolated pawn is so strategically significant that when defending against this you may have to resort to tactical means to get out of jail. Can you find the tactical idea that White can play here in order to maintain equality? } 1. { -KEY- } Nc7 $1 { Correct! Exploiting the fact that Black's e6 knight is tasked with guarding the bishop on c5. } ( 1. Bc4 $6 { Chasing the knight would sometimes work, but here, besides the move } Nef4 { , Black can also go for tactics with } Nxg3 $1 { when the point is that after } 2. hxg3 $6 ( 2. Qxd8 { was best, but after } Ne2 { Black would have ensured that White's isolated f-pawns remained on the board. } ) 
Qg5 $1 { White has no good way to meet the threats against the king. For example: } 3. Kh2 Rad8 $1 { The queen has no good square to run to, since either c2 or e2 will be met by ...Rd2. } 4. Qe1 ( { If } 4. Qb3 { then } Bxf2 $19 { wins on the spot, due to the hanging rook on c1. } ) 
Qh5 5. Kg2 Ng5 6. Rc3 Qh3 7. Kg1 Qxg3 $19 { with a winning attack. } ) 
Qg5 { Black tries the same idea, but now it is much less effective. } ( 1... Nxc7 2. Rxc5 Nxg3 { The double attack forces Black to 'correct' the opponent's structure. } 3. hxg3 $11 { White has no problems here, and in fact may stand slightly better as the bishop tends to be a more effective piece in open positions. } ) 
2. Nxe6 $1 { Damaging the structure, and removing one of Black's powerful knights targeting the f4 square. } ( 2. Nxa8 $2 { loses after } Nxg3 3. hxg3 Qxg3 4. Kh1 Qh3 5. Kg1 Nf4 $19 { Fittingly, the weak f4 square is what enables Black to land the killer blow. Mate on g2 cannot be stopped. } ) 
fxe6 3. Kh1 $14 ( { White will continue next with moves like Qc2 or Qb3, while } 3. Kh1 Nf4 { here is met by } 4. Be4 { when if anyone stands better it's White, since Black's light-squared pawns on b7 and e6 are even more fragile than White's kingside structure. So far we've looked at backward pawns, doubled pawns, and isolated pawns. These are the pawn types you'll encounter most often. Next up, we'll close out the section with one more pawn type that is closely related to the IQP. } ) 

