 { The final technique we'll cover has to do with targeting (or undermining) what are known as 'hanging pawns'. These pawns are characterised by the fact that they sit side-by-side on adjacent files, with no friendly pawns on neighboring files to protect them. In the position in front of you, Black has hanging pawns on c5 and d5. These pawns are neutral, meaning they can be good or bad depending on the position. The main advanage of possessing them is that they offer a lot of space and central control. Their main weakness is that, lacking the support of other pawns, they can be very vulnerable to attack. In this example and the next, we'll focus on how you should play against them. There are two main approaches you can take. The first is to apply as much piece pressure as possible to the pawns. The second is to find a pawn break to undermine the pawns. Oftentimes, the two approaches can be combined as happened in the following game. } 18. { -KEY- } Qc2 $1 { White has configured his pieces excellently. All the heavy pieces will soon point at the pawns, while the knight on f4 is already pressuring d5. } Nd7 19. Rfd1 $1 ( { Setting up the threat of } 19. Rfd1  20. Nxd5 { since after } Bxd5 21. Rxd5 $1 Qxd5 22. Bc4 { would win the queen. } ) 
d4 { A bad sign for the side with hanging pawns. When the hanging pawns advance they become imbalanced in their control over central squares. Black's pawns no longer guard the light squares, inviting the next strong move from White. } 20. Bc4 Bxc4 $6 ( 20... Ne5 { offered more resistance. Now White's pieces become even more active. } ) 
21. Qxc4+ Kh7 22. b4 $16 { White already had a near-winning position in Spassky,B - Pachman,L (1962). This example showcased both of the main ideas when facing hanging pawns: apply piece pressure, and aim for pawn breaks from the flank. Another good sign that you are taking the right approach is if you are able to force one of the hanging pawns to advance, as this causes them to lose a lot of their flexibility and strength. } 
