 { This position was reached in Euwe,M - Reshevsky,S (1953) . Black missed a strong pawn move here, can you spot it? } 28... { -KEY- } b5 $1 { Correct! The thematic pawn break is supported by tactics! } ( 28... Nd5 $5 { Funnily enough, the other strong approach in the position also involves using tactics. } 29. Rc2 Qe3 $1 30. Qf2 Qb3 $1 { The queen has maneuvered to b3, from where she is excellently placed to pressure the vulnerable c4 pawn. } ) 
29. cxb5 $6 ( 29. c5 $2 { loses to } b4 $19 { winning at least an exchange. } ) 
Rxc3 ( 29... Bxb5 $1 { is similar. The main difference is that in this variation Black would have had to spot that after } 30. Rxc8 { you must recapture on c8, when the point is that } Rxc8 31. Qxb5 $2 Qe3 $19 { wins for Black due to the combined threats of ...Qxf3 and ...Rc2 against the naked king. } ) 
30. Bxc3 Bxb5 31. Qd2 Qh5 32. Bg2 Qd5 $17 { Black stands much better here, having fractured White's hanging pawns and left him with an IQP instead. Given the limited pieces on the board, White enjoys none of the dynamic benefits that often come with an IQP while having all of the structural problems! This brings us to the end of the section. We covered seven popular techniques that will help you to improve your play whenever you encounter these common pawn types. Here's a quick recap of what we covered: (1) Backward pawns: fixing the structure (2) Doubled pawns: opportunities to undouble (3) Doubled pawns: splitting the pawn chain (4) Doubled pawns: the central pawn sacrifice (5) Isolated Queen's Pawn: exchanging pieces (6) Isolated pawn: fighting for the square in front of the pawn (7) Hanging pawns: how to attack and undermine them That's it for this pretty long chapter on pawn play - well done for working your way through it! Good luck in the puzzle section, and see you in the next chapter. } 
